Hint
You are given an array nums1 of n distinct integers.

You want to construct another array nums2 of length n such that the elements in nums2 are either all odd or all even.

For each index i, you must choose exactly one of the following (in any order):

nums2[i] = nums1[i]​​​​​​​
nums2[i] = nums1[i] - nums1[j], for an index j != i, such that nums1[i] - nums1[j] >= 1
Return true if it is possible to construct such an array, otherwise return false.

 

Example 1:

Input: nums1 = [1,4,7]

Output: true

Explanation:​​​​​​​​​​​​​​

Set nums2[0] = nums1[0] = 1.
Set nums2[1] = nums1[1] - nums1[0] = 4 - 1 = 3.
Set nums2[2] = nums1[2] = 7.
nums2 = [1, 3, 7], and all elements are odd. Thus, the answer is true.
Example 2:

Input: nums1 = [2,3]

Output: false

Explanation:

It is not possible to construct nums2 such that all elements have the same parity. Thus, the answer is false.

Example 3:

Input: nums1 = [4,6]

Output: true

Explanation:

Set nums2[0] = nums1[0] = 4.
Set nums2[1] = nums1[1] = 6.
nums2 = [4, 6], and all elements are even. Thus, the answer is true.
class Solution {
public:
    bool uniformArray(vector<int>& nums1) {
        int n=nums1.size(),nums2[n];
        for(int i=0;i<n;i++){
            nums2[i]=nums1[i];
            for(int j=i;j<n;j++){
                if(nums2[i]%2==0 && i!=j){
                    if(nums1[i] - nums1[j] >= 1){
                        nums2[i]=nums1[i] - nums1[j];
                    }
                }
                else if(nums2[i]%2!=0&&i!=j){
                    if(nums1[i] - nums1[j] >= 1){
                        nums2[i]=nums1[i] - nums1[j];
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        bool even=false;
        if(nums2[0]%2==0){
            even=true;
        }
        if(even){
            for(int i:nums2){
            if(i%2!=0){
                return false;
            }
        }
        }
        if(!even){
            for(int i:nums2){
            if(i%2==0){
                return false;
            }
        }
        }
        return true;
    }
};

How "Construct Uniform Parity Array II" 
actually worksIn this problem, you can only transform an element by subtracting a strictly smaller
element from it (\(nums1[i] - nums1[j] \ge 1\)).
To make an element even: You must start with an even number, or subtract an odd number from 
an odd number (Odd - Odd = Even). Therefore, you can only make everything even if there are no odd numbers in the array to begin with.
To make an element odd: You must start with an odd number, or subtract an odd number from an even number (Even - Odd = Odd). Therefore, every 
even number needs a smaller odd number to subtract from.This means you can make everything odd as long as the absolute smallest number in the 
entire array is an odd number.

class Solution {
public:
    bool uniformArray(vector<int>& nums1) {
        int min_element = INT_MAX;
        bool has_odd = false;
        
        // Find the absolute minimum value and see if any odd numbers exist
        for (int x : nums1) {
            min_element = min(min_element, x);
            if (x % 2 != 0) {
                has_odd = true;
            }
        }
        
        // 1. If there are no odd numbers, it's already uniformly even.
        // 2. If there are odd numbers, the smallest number in the array MUST be odd.
        return !has_odd || (min_element % 2 != 0);
    }
};
